Idle Timeout in an AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB)?
Here is the Quick Answer to it 

🎯 ALB has a setting called Idle Timeout.
🎯 It defines how long ALB keeps a TCP/HTTP connection open when there is no activity (no data being sent/received).
🎯 After this time passes, ALB closes the connection.
===============================================

🔹 Example
Suppose Idle Timeout = 60 seconds (default).
A client (browser or app) connects to your service through ALB.
If no request/response happens for 60 seconds, the ALB closes the connection.
Next request → client must re-establish a new connection.
===========================================
🔹 Why It Matters
Too low timeout (e.g., 10s):
 Long requests (like file uploads, API calls, DB queries) may fail because ALB closes the connection too soon.
Too high timeout (e.g., 600s):
 Connections remain open even if idle → waste resources.
===============================================
🔹 Common Settings
Default: 60 seconds.
