AWS ALB Interview Q&A
1. What is an Application Load Balancer (ALB)?
ALB is a Layer 7 load balancer that distributes incoming HTTP/HTTPS traffic across multiple targets (EC2, containers, Lambda). It supports path-based and host-based routing.

What is path based and Host based Routing ?
🔹 Path-based routing (URL path)
 Think of it like a mall:
If you go to /foodcourt, the security guard sends you to the food area.
If you go to /cinema, you get sent to the movie hall.
Similarly, ALB looks at the path in the URL and sends the request to the right service.
Example:
example.com/api/* → API servers
example.com/app/* → Frontend servers
🔹 Host-based routing (domain name)
 Now imagine the same mall has multiple entrances with names:
food.example.com goes directly to the food court.
shop.example.com goes to the shopping area.
ALB checks the host/domain name in the request and routes it to the correct backend.
👉 So in short:
Path-based = depends on what comes after the slash (/) in the URL.
Host-based = depends on the domain/subdomain name.
